  "body": {
    "id": "prop:bounded-pooled-two-arm-sanity",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "On the bounded restricted schedule class\n\\(Y_i^{[L,U]}(2)=Y_i^{[L,U]}(3)\\) for every \\(i\\), pooling labels \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) under \\(q^\\star\\) produces balanced two-arm assignment and the causal target becomes the active-arm mean minus the pooled-comparator mean.  The endpoint subclass is represented by the published shifted-binomial experiment, while the entire bounded pooled problem has minimax risk \\(W^2\\) times that binary value.  Its second-order deficit coefficient is therefore \\(W^2(-4^{1/3}a_1')\\).  This is a value transfer; \\(P_m(x\\mid r)\\) is not a literal likelihood for a nonendpoint bounded schedule.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Y_i^{[L,U]}(a)",
      "i",
      "q^\\star",
      "W",
      "a_1'",
      "P_m(x\\mid r)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "prop:pooled-two-arm-sanity",
      "lem:published-two-arm-experimental-reductions",
      "lem:published-two-arm-bounded-endpoint-transfer",
      "lem:published-two-arm-sharp-constant",
      "def:bounded-games"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Write the common comparator outcome as \\(Y_i^{[L,U]}(0)\\).  Since\n\\(q_1^\\star=1/2\\) and \\(q_2^\\star+q_3^\\star=1/2\\), pooling the two comparator labels maps \\(\\mathcal D_{q^\\star}\\) to iid balanced two-arm assignment.  Conditional on the pooled label, the split between labels \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) is ancillary because their potential outcomes agree.  Moreover,\n\\[\n\\tau_{[L,U]}(z^{[L,U]})\n=\\frac1n\\sum_i\\left\\{Y_i^{[L,U]}(1)-Y_i^{[L,U]}(0)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nOn endpoint schedules, normalization by \\(W\\) gives the balanced binary two-arm problem.  The cited Theorems 2.1--2.2 identify its minimax value with the shifted-binomial value, and the cited Theorem 2.3 gives the exact factor \\(W^2\\) over the full bounded interval.  The cited sharp two-arm result gives the binary coefficient \\(-4^{1/3}a_1'\\), so rescaling gives \\(W^2(-4^{1/3}a_1')\\).  The endpoint-transfer proof proceeds by averaging endpoint experiments and therefore does not identify a nonendpoint bounded schedule with an eight-type count.",
